            "[[wernicke-encephalopathy|Wernicke encephalopathy]] is an acute brain disorder caused by lack of [[thiamine|thiamine]] (vitamin B1). The body stores little thiamine, so weeks of poor intake or vomiting can exhaust it.\n\nAlcohol use disorder is the best-known setting, but the Lancet Neurology review by Sechi and Serra stresses the others: bariatric surgery, hyperemesis gravidarum, prolonged vomiting from any cause, malnutrition, cancer, prolonged intravenous feeding without vitamins, and refeeding after starvation.\n\nThe classic triad is confusion (or other change in mental state), eye signs ([[nystagmus|nystagmus]] or weakness of eye movements, often lateral gaze), and [[ataxia|ataxia]], mainly of stance and gait. The full triad is present in only a minority of patients, which is a major reason the diagnosis is missed and is often made only at autopsy. For patients with alcohol use disorder, the EFNS guideline suggests the clinical diagnosis needs two of four features: dietary deficiency, eye signs, cerebellar dysfunction, and an altered mental state or mild memory impairment.",
