          body:
            "Metabolic [[encephalopathy|encephalopathy]] usually causes a diffuse picture: inattention, fluctuating confusion, drowsiness, and sometimes [[asterixis|asterixis]] or [[myoclonus|myoclonus]]. The exceptions matter.\n\n[[hypoglycemia|Hypoglycemia]] can cause focal deficits such as hemiparesis or aphasia, as well as confusion, seizures, and coma. This is why glucose is checked in every suspected stroke and every patient with altered consciousness, before imaging decisions are made.\n\n[[hyponatremia|Hyponatremia]] causes nausea, headache, confusion, seizures, and coma, more so when the fall is rapid. In chronic hyponatremia the brain adapts by shedding solutes from its cells, so it tolerates the low sodium. If the sodium is then corrected too fast, water leaves the adapted brain cells and they shrink, which can cause [[osmotic-demyelination-syndrome|osmotic demyelination syndrome]]. It typically appears days after the correction, with dysarthria, dysphagia, weakness of all four limbs, and sometimes a locked-in state. Alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, low potassium, and liver disease raise the risk.\n\nThe 2014 European clinical practice guideline on hyponatraemia (Spasovski and colleagues) advises limiting the rise in serum sodium to 10 mmol/L in the first 24 hours and 8 mmol/L in each 24 hours after that, with frequent sodium checks. Severe symptoms such as seizures or coma still need urgent treatment to raise the sodium a little; the danger lies in going too far, not in starting.",
