          body:
            "[[hepatic-encephalopathy|Hepatic encephalopathy]] is brain dysfunction from liver failure or portosystemic shunting. It ranges from subtle slowing and sleep reversal to coma, and asterixis is a classic sign. Look for a precipitant: infection, gastrointestinal bleeding, constipation, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, or sedative drugs.\n\nUremic encephalopathy, from advanced kidney failure, also causes confusion with asterixis and myoclonus.\n\nToxic causes to recognize:\n\n- Lithium toxicity: tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, confusion, and myoclonus. It can be triggered by dehydration or by drugs that reduce lithium clearance, such as NSAIDs, thiazides, and ACE inhibitors.\n- Carbon monoxide: headache, dizziness, and confusion, sometimes in several members of one household. Standard pulse oximetry can read normal, so carboxyhemoglobin must be measured. Delayed neuropsychiatric problems can follow.\n- [[serotonin-syndrome|Serotonin syndrome]] and [[neuroleptic-malignant-syndrome|neuroleptic malignant syndrome]], described next.\n\nBoyer and Shannon's NEJM review describes serotonin syndrome as an excess of serotonergic activity, from therapeutic use, overdose, or a drug interaction. It develops quickly, over hours, with agitation or confusion, autonomic signs (fever, sweating, fast heart rate, dilated pupils, diarrhea), and neuromuscular excitation: [[clonus|clonus]] (inducible, spontaneous, or ocular), [[hyperreflexia|hyperreflexia]], and tremor, more marked in the legs. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows a dopamine antagonist (or abrupt withdrawal of dopaminergic drugs) and evolves over days, with severe generalized lead-pipe rigidity, slow movement, reduced reflexes, high fever, and altered consciousness.",
